uid "ac6e1532-4a17-5c1a-b959-d5f981528176" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161616 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161616 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161616 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b629b10a-e7f8-56d9-8e10-0dd66fa5859b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161617 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161617 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161617 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c92890c5-c66c-5b6e-991c-d638b43650a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161803 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161803 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2 = 20.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161803 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42777bd2-44d0-5117-9e8b-ee722d8f3229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161803 #edb/ref 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2.$ The matrix of the form is \n\n![](images/31/31f8c54fecdb8b2e8b3f3cad20a642a39eab9ae2987d49849a1c1dabc34e5368.png)\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 20\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I| = 0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=20 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=10.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=20,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix}-20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/51/51897cac20d424f599eba1c8aada5259279ab9ad263e7557d6614ce79e68fa0f.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n![](images/f9/f9913f9139af447be912bccc2350f086ce32bae89849495f8e82a3eebf8a7918.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/2c/2cdd8527be173b17e254bd4ecda571d71b15938c3a1f6cb2609122db2b10c64d.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n![](images/f6/f6764ef3200afce932740e8100915018b6a0c21e902d932b218e79bcd57e0b95.png)\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/15/1549a73afb90f3fa0fb8218d9d63daa1a5534c3e546613cbf3f226371b8f1092.png) and ![](images/d4/d4a6e3f0c138e6dd83252df8adc14359ba67ae77dec0270264ea6d00c7b08667.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186161804 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161804 #edb/ref 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation $6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2= 70?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161804 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3564c452-1a51-5455-a6b1-223fda1611c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161804 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2.$ To  determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ which is\n\n![](images/33/33a30f2c07875359a60caed74f802a2de29ac9e44089800ed8f30a2015303bce.png)\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}-10{y}_{2}^{2} & =70 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161815 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161815 #edb/ref 1071 "Draw a diagram that depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown below.\n\n![](images/53/5354b2fa6d2fb2c8b1b9ea3cb675225619e8debe64e1ec108b556248d6e672ec.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161815 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec194ea7-df30-5370-b0c4-d27d4b34f8c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161815 #edb/ref 1074 "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](images/bd/bd0d117543012be1c4eab0acda878c9aea67518e46c93048e8c249de1a0c03e6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161838 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8678" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161838 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix ![](images/41/4114e6536f57106949ed39a6ae4979646a0dd58c5690d7e7bcf9ffed15de8086.png) and the quadratic curve $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=1,$ find the principal axes of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161838 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba46e82f-10b0-529c-94b0-3aebb7d1e1a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161838 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/77/771d020606de64641ab305039a0c9a9de926080e83707b270a333b5775a8e028.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/54/54a77383e863630dfd4e21a796801001aaed3084f1097bca62ef4a098e20b405.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/e0/e0d71ad9216b9fa7c1831e6874b9a97bba9c1d9c5dbd6d9664635ed468d82a4c.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/83/83fd398b476c1c749346754a05c7f4071a98ab61453405a99ad8fcff65e38aaf.png)\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/8f/8f0d84bb5c3ae37714c781b5dafbba1ae7de8561234ca558d3da2dcbfa9d75cf.png) and ![](images/29/293ad98697d595e3e0049aae93e54209b6d17aa92eb131b57791f211167690fc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186162011 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162011 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162011 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162011 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162011 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162011 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363182 