#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360271 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360271 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360271 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186359753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360271 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a08665b1-cc8f-56e9-9105-4ca0c202d740" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360314 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360314 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360314 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360314 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360314 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360314 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360314 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360314 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186359260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360314 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b687ab4c-a299-5984-a73e-2a55596c2040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360546 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}=100.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82f29e7a-6602-5687-8c17-e73a53aea769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360546 #edb/ref 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=100\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=25\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=20.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-25I & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{bmatrix}-25\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-25I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=20,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{bmatrix}-20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360631 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360631 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360631 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360631 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360631 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e61ec711-f880-5e14-a323-327181d18534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360631 #edb/ref 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=9\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360702 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360702 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96727" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360702 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360702 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e6/e6a6d3891e514f31c77ccbf098d06843bbcd771f2ebea83861683584ec5e8805.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360702 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e3c2cae-6be7-5f78-a826-46459817fa90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360702 #edb/ref 1074 "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is an ellipse. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif the axes pass through the opposite vertices of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/d0/d06b0e6d93c3c0679aea11512963e19c2e641f506558f60c185624de2464c451.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361140 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186361140 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361140 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "46150034-989c-516f-93b6-095ee6a1128f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361394 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186361394 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361394 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7e9550b3-3b43-558a-b687-941423083fde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361512 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186361512 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361512 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "974bf80c-567c-5781-bb2e-8dc105925a14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361527 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186361527 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361527 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9af896d3-e35f-5f59-b9a5-41d2e1a7b105" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361621 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186361621 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361621 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b0d6ae70-5ded-5400-bdbb-443f404b013a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362166 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362166 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362166 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362166 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362166 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362166 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362166 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362166 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362166 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c94d3a5d-99de-5470-b87a-285f7cc8b266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362260 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362260 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97993" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362260 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362260 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=3.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ find the principal axes of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362260 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b21820d5-a22a-56c9-af9c-9c1893fbf413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362260 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=3\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-7)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-7)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363189 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363189 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363189 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=11.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ find the principal axes of the curve." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "778909b3-51d4-53f8-86d3-fe36ffdc7654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363189 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=11\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-5)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-5)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363190 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363190 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363190 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363190 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363190 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363190 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363190 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #e